\ifelse{html}{\href{https://lifecycle.r-lib.org/articles/stages.html#experimental}{\figure{lifecycle-experimental.svg}{options: alt='[Experimental]'}}}{\strong{[Experimental]}}

Daily surveillance data is often too sparse to nowcast: most (event date,
report date) cells hold a zero or a one, and the delay distribution is mostly
noise. The usual fix is to work in weeks instead. \code{aggregate_time_units()}
does that in one call -- it moves every date onto the coarser grid, adds the
counts up, and returns a \code{tbl_now} that \emph{knows} it is now weekly, so
\code{.delay}, the converters and the models all count in weeks from then on.
}
\details{
Each date is replaced by the \strong{start of the period it falls in}: the Sunday
that begins its epidemiological week, the first of its month, the first of
January of its year (\code{label = "end"} names the period by its last day
instead). Weeks go through the same epi/ISO machinery \code{\link[=align_weeks]{align_weeks()}} uses,
so \code{type} and \code{align_on_day} mean exactly what they mean there.

What happens to the rows depends on the data type:
\itemize{
\item \strong{\code{linelist}} -- one row is still one case; only the dates move.
\item \strong{\code{count-incidence}} -- rows that land in the same (event, report) cell are
summed.
\item \strong{\code{count-cumulative}} -- cumulative totals are \strong{not} additive, so the
series is de-accumulated to increments first, aggregated, and accumulated
again on the new grid.
}

Two consequences worth knowing:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Weeks do not nest inside months.} Aggregating daily data to weeks and
\emph{then} to months is not the same as going straight to months: the second
pass sees only the week's label, so a week beginning 31 December lands in
December even though most of its cases happened in January. Aggregate once,
to the unit you actually want.
\item An \code{NA} count means \emph{not yet observed}, so a period containing one has an
unknown total, not a total that quietly leaves it out. Use
\code{\link[=complete_zeroes]{complete_zeroes()}} first if the \code{NA}s are really zeroes.
}

Any temporal-effect \strong{columns} that were materialised by
\code{\link[=compute_temporal_effects]{compute_temporal_effects()}} are dropped, because a day-of-week term computed
on daily dates is meaningless once those dates are weeks. The lazy
\code{\link[=temporal_effects]{temporal_effects()}} spec is kept, so \code{compute_temporal_effects()} will
rebuild them on the new grid.
}
\section{Aggregating one axis only}{

\code{axes} exists because the two axes do not always move together: a system may
record the day a specimen was taken but only publish weekly report batches.
Two things follow:
\itemize{
\item \code{tbl_now()} requires the report axis to be \strong{at least as coarse} as the
event axis, so aggregating only the event axis of a daily object is
refused. Aggregate the report axis too, or use \code{axes = "all"}.
\item A week named by the day it \emph{starts} sits before every date inside it, so
coarsening a \strong{later} axis alone -- the report against a daily event, or
the validation against a daily report -- with \code{label = "start"} produces
negative delays, and \code{\link[=validate_tbl_now]{validate_tbl_now()}} warns. Use \code{label = "end"}: a
report that arrived somewhere in week \emph{W} is known by the end of \emph{W}, which
is the honest bound. When the axes move together the labelling cancels out
and either choice gives the same delays.
